    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A kingdom of the Yemeni highlands, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zafar-yemen">Zafar</a>, that united all of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a> around 300 CE.</li><li>Around 380 its kings gave up the old gods for one god, the Merciful, in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#monotheism">monotheism</a> close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#judaism">Judaism</a>.</li><li>Conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#najran">Christian Najran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> ended in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kaleb">Kaleb</a>’s invasion of 525, in which the last king, Yusuf, was killed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Himyarites dated their inscriptions by their own era, which began around 110 BCE, so many Himyarite texts can be dated to the exact year.</p></aside>
    </section>

